# The @stackmyth/* UI packages are published privately to GitHub Packages, not
# npmjs. This scope mapping is safe to commit and is required on a fresh clone
# and in CI — without it the installer looks in the wrong registry and fails.
@stackmyth:registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com/

# The credential is deliberately NOT here.
#
# pnpm refuses to expand environment variables in auth lines that come from a
# project-level .npmrc, precisely because this file is committed and a leaked
# token would be handed to whatever registry the file names. It warns and
# ignores the line. npm would expand it, so keeping it would "work" for npm and
# silently not for pnpm — worse than not having it.
#
# Put the token somewhere the installer will trust instead:
#
#   Locally — in your user-level ~/.npmrc:
#     @stackmyth:registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com/
#     //npm.pkg.github.com/:_authToken=ghp_your_read_packages_token
#
#   or:  pnpm config set "//npm.pkg.github.com/:_authToken" ghp_...
#
#   On Vercel — set the NPM_RC environment variable to those two lines;
#   Vercel writes it to ~/.npmrc before installing.
#
#   In GitHub Actions — actions/setup-node with `registry-url` and
#   `scope: '@stackmyth'`, plus NODE_AUTH_TOKEN on the install step.
#
#   NOT the built-in GITHUB_TOKEN, which is what this comment used to say and
#   what left CI broken. That token can only read packages owned by THIS
#   repository's owner: junti is in `vennet-developers`, the @stackmyth
#   packages are published from `stackmyth/stackmyth`, and a cross-org read
#   fails with ERR_PNPM_FETCH_401 no matter what `permissions:` says. It needs
#   a classic PAT with `read:packages`, stored as the STACKMYTH_NPM_TOKEN
#   repository secret. See .github/workflows/ci.yml.
